WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.323
- But why don't we go ahead

00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:09.320
and get started with our Bellos lecture?

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.800
This is such a highlight
for the year for the school.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.850
It's such a part of our tradition

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.650
and we've had such wonderful
speakers over the years,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.160
and today is certainly no exception.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.570
We then always have, of course,
the reception afterwards,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.760
the wizard tea.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.060
And of course, there's history
and story to that as well.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.780
And Sten is happy about the
tea because he's an Anglophile,

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.290
but we will have the reception following.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.430
It's such an honor and a personal pleasure

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.050
for me to be able to introduce
to you Dr. Sten Vermund,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.500
Dean of the School of Public Health.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.320
Sten and I go way, way back
when I was a little student

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.990
and he was a baby faculty
back in New York City,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.335
back in the days when the
HIV pandemic was starting.

00:00:53.335 --> 00:00:58.335
Sten is a Yale and national resource,

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.350
trained as a pediatrician,
obviously a public health expert,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.480
and I'm gonna say an honorary nurse.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.260
He has always gotten the
power of public health

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.530
and nursing working together.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.410
Since done it all, okay, so sure.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.840
He's an infectious disease epidemiologist,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.350
we all knew that, we've
all seen him on the media,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.860
and he's helped Yale respond
to COVID during this pandemic,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.270
he's a pediatrician by
training and practice.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.140
And I think those of you
who are pediatricians

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.690
and adolescent medicine will appreciate

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:30.800
that clinical perspective.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.800
He's focused on improving
care in every setting,

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.473
I'm gonna say.

00:01:37.473 --> 00:01:40.380
He has worked in multiple
countries around the world,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.300
always thinking about
how do we get to equity,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.210
which, of course, is our theme
at Yale School of Nursing.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.610
He's worked on HIV, HPV,
TB, parasitic diseases,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.160
and of course, coronavirus,

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.130
just a handful of the pathogens

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.940
that he has brought his
bearing and his expertise to

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.280
and collaborations with so
many people around the world.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.380
He is interested in interventional science

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.950
and he led the HIV prevention
trials network for many years,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.780
a huge endeavor.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.590
And so that has really moved the needle

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.220
on a lot of our HIV
prevention clinical trials

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.490
and implementation science,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.630
which is a theme also here at Yale.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.240
And it's always about, as
we care about as nurses,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.623
increasing coverage, quality,
evidence, and impact of care.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.340
He has, as I say, experience
in the real world,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.080
in academia, and also in NIH.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.100
And so those of you who
may have been around

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.453
during the HIV pandemic,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.310
he was one of those
incredible bridge people

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.480
who could work with the activists,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.220
people living with HIV, who were dying,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.030
who were trying to make change

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:49.580
in how do we do science so
that people can survive.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.610
He came up with the
first as women's cohort,

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.410
HIV women's cohort.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.110
I think I remember you being
on stage with Jim Curran

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.450
and the activists were throwing
toilet paper at you all

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.140
and you just kept speaking,
you heard the concerns,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.890
and change was made in
the way we do science

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.950
and what you did at NIH,
very, very incredible.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.400
Of course, in this most recent period,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.520
as Sten has been leading
the School of Public Health,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.530
our sister school,

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:18.170
he also pivoted towards COVID-19 research,

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.290
including working with
collaborators in China

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.090
and around the world.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.820
He worked with Yale Kamis here.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.200
These are just some examples on mask use,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.780
safety with schools and arts organizations

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.370
in New York City, New York,
as well as in Connecticut.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.180
And he has really continued all that work

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.760
along with his incredible
faculty who are collaborators

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.150
of many of ours at
School of Public Health.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.870
He's, of course, has reached
the pinnacle of achievements

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.850
in being recognized for his
science and his contribution.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.990
He's elected member of the
National Academy of Medicine,

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.290
fellow of the American Association

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.620
for the Advancement of Science,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.220
I'm sure missing a lot of accolades.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.690
But really, the most important thing

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.690
is he's a genuinely kind and
smart and warm collaborator.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.440
He champions people,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.470
he wants everyone to be doing good work,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.980
and he helps support us all on doing that,

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.670
including thinking about nursing

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:11.950
and public health partnerships.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.400
So he's embodied that as the dean

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.890
of the School of Public Health.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.900
He was instrumental with us as we formed

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.220
the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.960
Remembering that the three of our schools

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:21.970
really launched that.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.070
And so I didn't announce
this to you before, Sten,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.320
but rather than getting you
a Yale bowl or Yale pen,

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.810
we're going to plant a tree for you.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.459
- [Sten] Oh, lovely.

00:04:31.459 --> 00:04:32.292
- And that will be our gift to you,

00:04:32.292 --> 00:04:34.040
and thank you for speaking today,

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.370
particularly given the topic,

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.030
which is the topic of our time,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.920
and that tree someday will grow
and shelter, and be felled,

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.440
and turned into a bowl for someone else.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.413
But for you, it'll be the tree.

00:04:44.413 --> 00:04:45.517
Thank you, Sten.
- Thank you.

00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:48.167
Thank you, Ann. Thank you very much.

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:51.800
(audience applauding)

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.090
Well, that was very kind, very generous,

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.550
and I'm really thrilled to be here.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:01.310
I came shortly after my arrival
in 2017 over to the school,

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.650
marveled at your space, salivated,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.220
and was really impressed
with what you were doing

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.053
in the school.

00:05:09.053 --> 00:05:12.320
That was the time you were
doing your simulation labs,

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.380
you were building them,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.000
and there was just so much
vibrancy and excitement

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.690
in the school, and there
still is to this day.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.690
So I'm bringing a relatively
familiar topic to you,

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.670
so I hope I can give it some novelty

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.640
and a little bit of perspective.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.680
So what we're going to
try to do is take a look

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.913
at the latest data I could find,

00:05:36.227 --> 00:05:41.227
and just remind you that
prior climatologic predictions

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.240
from green house gas release

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.730
have actually understated true effects.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.770
This is if we've learned
anything in the last 12 months,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.080
it's that all of these dire predictions,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.380
it's the upper 95% confidence
interval that we're tracking,

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.270
not the middle, certainly not the low.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.440
So we'll review a few
climatological highlights,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:07.440
discuss mitigation efforts,
implications for human health,

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:11.223
for example, strategies for
adaptation to planetary warming,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.993
relevance for loss of
habitat, species diversity,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:18.090
comment on human and population growth,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.490
and will end with a comment
about nursing as a vital element

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:22.920
for climate change adaptation.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.920
Now it took me, I'm not
quite the guru in this topic,

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.970
but Ann is, and I would model my use

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.100
of the word mitigation and adaptation.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.400
I'm gonna put it out there
just because I learned it.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:41.990
Now, if I'm a carbon capture engineer,

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:46.880
or if I'm somebody working
on policy to guide us

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.320
to set our thermostats properly,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.120
or to massively improve solar power,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.040
or improve the efficiency of machinery

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.670
so it uses less energy,

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.613
then I'm mitigating climate change.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.840
But if I'm just a pediatrician

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:05.420
with a background in infectious
disease epidemiology,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.420
and I'm looking at island
nations that are being inundated,

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:15.140
or I'm looking at malaria
zones that are expanding

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:19.250
and I'm trying to help cope
with that, that's adaptation.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:22.480
So what we really want
is we want mitigation,

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:23.320
but guess what?

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.850
We, nurses, public health
professionals, docs,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.910
and health providers have
to worry about adaptation,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:30.880
'cause that's our space,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.670
that's the space where we think

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.340
we can actually make a difference.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.990
So let me make sure I
can get this to work.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.410
And here you've seen
this before, many of you.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:47.410
From 1880 to 2021, the more
yellow and the more red,

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:54.250
the greater the deviance
from historic norms.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:55.860
And take a look at the poles,

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.280
how much red pops up at the poles.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.280
And I guess we're at 1990
now and we're coming to 2000

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.443
and 2010 and 2021.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.900
Oops, didn't mean to show it twice.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:19.420
Okay, so the annual mean
surface temperature anomaly

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.270
is what the meteorologist refer to

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.340
when they're doing these historic

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:27.290
temperature gradient comparisons.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.500
And the past eight years
have been the warmest

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.140
in recorded history

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.330
as per the World
Meteorological Organization,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:36.850
and as per everybody else.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:38.650
No matter who is analyzing this,

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.010
they all say the same thing
even with different methods.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.690
The last eight years have been

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.160
modern history's warmest eight years.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.910
There are record levels of heat-trapping

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.200
atmospheric greenhouse gases.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.360
The global temperature about 1.1 degrees

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.340
higher than historic norms.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.810
And you know that we're
trying with climate change

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:02.960
to keep it to under 1.5

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.300
and we're not gonna get there actually,

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:12.300
but we need to work today to
make sure we keep it under two.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.240
So this is the seventh consecutive year

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:18.440
where global temperature

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.410
has been more than one degree celsius

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.640
above pre-industrial levels.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.630
And you know the consequences are massive,

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:31.160
loss of sea ice and ice
sheet mass, sea level rise.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.940
We've got longer, more intense heat waves

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.570
and major habitat changes affecting humans

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.740
and animals and plants alike.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.660
And I'm just straining to see.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:46.620
So the upper map is global
temperature trends, 1901 to 2017.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:53.550
And the lower map is 1988 to 2017.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:58.483
So just to give you a sense
of the comparative analysis.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:03.950
And the greenhouse gas trends
are unremittingly grim,

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:05.293
up, up, up.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:10.417
So here's another fancy,
Sten can barely cope video.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:19.180
Okay, so now we start in 2002

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:23.070
and we take a look at the
Greenland ice mass loss

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:26.430
over about a 19-year period,

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.763
and will be coming up to 2020.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:33.610
And this is Greenland ice loss

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:38.270
and meters equivalent relative to 2002.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.100
And when we're measuring here,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:44.280
this Y axis is mass loss in gigatons.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.130
Now I personally have
no concept of a gigaton

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.173
other than it's a heck of a lot of ice.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.240
That's my technical
interpretation of a gigaton.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.220
It's a massive amount of ice.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:01.220
And one more time, and
this is Antarctic ice mass,

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.573
that was Greenland,
now we have Antarctica,

00:11:06.265 --> 00:11:10.220
and this is from 2002 to 2019.

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:16.500
And taking another look at 150 gigatons

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.240
of lost ice mass per year.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:24.240
What happens is that
these glacial features

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.210
are almost like the stopper on a bottle.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:33.210
And when you take the
stopper off by melting,

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.411
and you might have the separation

00:11:35.411 --> 00:11:38.540
of a distal part of the glacier,

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:41.090
the rest of the glacier
starts to move even faster.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.750
You've taken that bottle
off or that bottle top off,

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.630
so that's what we're facing.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:50.140
And you may be aware
that we had about two,

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:52.452
was it two, three years ago, Ann?

00:11:52.452 --> 00:11:57.452
The calving of a huge
component of Antarctica,

00:12:01.852 --> 00:12:04.835
the size of the state of Rhode Island.

00:12:04.835 --> 00:12:09.835
Now that did not do much
for global sea level rise,

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.530
because it was already
essentially floating,

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.590
but it's separated now
and now it will melt,

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:18.390
and then it will contribute.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:21.971
And we're running into massive shifts

00:12:21.971 --> 00:12:25.370
that the climatologists
really can't even believe.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.080
Now, that's just Mount Kilimanjaro,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:31.010
the tallest mountain in Africa.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:35.140
If you're my age, you actually
read Hemingway as a kid,

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.700
these short stories, and
then you saw the movie

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:42.700
with Gregory Peck, and Susan
Hayward, and Ava Gardner,

00:12:42.777 --> 00:12:44.870
"The Snows of Kilimanjaro."

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.700
There are no more snows on Kilimanjaro.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:52.700
So you look at 1912, you
look at 1970, 2000, and 2007,

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:59.000
and this is schematically
represented over to the right.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.870
And the snow mass on
the top of Kilimanjaro

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:04.760
is relatively trivial.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:09.113
As a consequence in the
warming of the entire region,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.750
this used to be the part of Kenya

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:15.130
where the colonials would
go to a avoid malaria

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.670
in the hot season.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:18.820
Well, now you have malaria all the way up,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.670
halfway up Kilimanjaro,
you can get malaria,

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:26.170
and that's because of expanded
vector-breeding opportunities

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:28.660
because it's warmer and moisture.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:31.000
There's also been a lot of deforestation.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:32.810
And so you throw that in

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:36.063
and you have a lot of
trouble in this region.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.830
Now, the most terrifying
of all the slides,

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:43.660
we'll get to the optimistic slides later,

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.690
that's what we're gonna all do about this.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.460
But the most terrifying slide is this one,

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.920
the thermohaline circulation,

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:51.510
particularly in the Atlantic current,

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.360
the so-called meridional
overturning circulation

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:55.560
or the MOC.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:00.560
Now these are the giant
rivers within the oceans,

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:02.980
if you wanna look at it that way.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.230
So deep down, you have
cold water currents,

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:10.300
they rise as they come into the tropics,

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.130
and then they return
as warm water currents.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:16.870
The most famous of which
is the Gulf Stream.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.360
So if you look at the Gulf Stream up here,

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.920
take a look at just how far north,

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:27.920
I think I have a pointer, I can't see it.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:30.990
Anyway, you know where Norway is,

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:34.800
where my parents came from, and Iceland,

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.260
and take a look at how far north that is.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:40.853
That's Greenland, that's
Siberia, that's Northern Canada.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:44.530
Now why can people live
in Iceland and Norway?

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.140
It's all about the Gulf Stream.

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.930
That's why those are habitable countries.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:53.180
They're way too far north to be habitable

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.450
in a productive way.

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.580
So what's happened is for the first time

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:00.000
in a recorded history,

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.540
we've measured the
slowing of these currents

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:06.960
and nobody on Planet Earth has a clue

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.890
as to what that might mean.

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:11.800
We do know that there
has systematic warming

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:17.530
and bleaching of reefs because of warming,

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:20.380
so that corals are adapted
to slightly cooler water,

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.020
you warm it up, they die.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:23.700
And it's also true that we've seen

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:27.403
massive displacements of fish populations

00:15:27.403 --> 00:15:31.760
as they seek cooler waters closer

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.767
to their millennially
adapted physiologies.

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:42.440
So this could be the most
important consequence

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:44.440
of global climate change

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.960
if we actually see the
substantial disruption

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.620
of these currents because
we really don't know

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:53.290
how this could disrupt the
ecosystems of the globe.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:56.273
So here are the health issues,
direct effects of heat.

00:15:57.952 --> 00:16:00.130
We have hotter and drier summers

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.420
and we can have heat-related deaths.

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.030
We have warmer and wet winters

00:16:05.506 --> 00:16:08.360
with the consequence of loss
of polar and glacial ice.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:11.150
We have increased extreme weather events,

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:13.840
we have property and crop loss,

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.630
and we have loss of reef ecosystems,

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:18.130
which disrupts fish yield.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:21.830
So you can see the direct
effects on our food chain

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:25.330
and especially among the
elderly and the very young

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:28.150
who are much more susceptible
to things like heatstroke.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:32.020
And you may recall in Europe,
it was about 15 years ago,

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:35.520
there was a massive
atypical European heat wave.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:40.520
And it's estimated that
20,000 or so elderly in Europe

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.840
whose air conditioners were not working

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:44.650
or who didn't have air conditioners,

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:46.830
and they called the air
conditioning supply company,

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.540
and nobody came because
they were overwhelmed

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:49.700
with other calls

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:51.760
and they essentially
cooked in their apartments.

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.680
And that has happened a
lesser degree in the US,

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.270
but it's the kind of thing
that we can anticipate.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:02.230
So are we, as nurses, in this case,

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.660
maybe not pediatrician, but for the kids.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:06.030
Are we as geriatricians,

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:10.353
are we going to start taking seriously

00:17:10.353 --> 00:17:13.110
how well networked our elderly are

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:15.000
in time of stress and emergency?

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:19.210
Are we going to actually have
plans to cope with heat waves

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:23.680
and the inevitability that
one in 20 air conditioners

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.330
gonna break down during this period

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:26.340
and isn't gonna be fixed

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:28.110
because the companies are overwhelmed?

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:29.210
Are we going to plan for this?

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.960
These are the sorts of adaptation issues

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:32.840
that we have to think about.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.470
And there are plenty
of direct heat effects

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.260
that we're going to have to worry about.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:39.050
Perhaps, the most dramatic example

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:41.750
of a direct heat effect
is desertification.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.910
So the Sahara Desert is moving south

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:47.800
in the Sahel region of Africa,

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.343
at least 100 kilometers per year,

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.250
and that is displacing
the grazers and farmers

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:57.610
who have lived in these
Sahel border regions

00:17:57.610 --> 00:17:59.790
with the desert.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.490
And this is the kind of
phenomenon you'll see

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:03.323
when you go there.

00:18:03.323 --> 00:18:05.650
People having abandoned
their dying animals

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:09.700
and having to migrate, tremendous stress,

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:12.980
and if the vegetation animals
aren't going to survive,

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:14.333
the people have to move.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.550
Now we have this
climatologic displacement,

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:20.650
climate refugees that I just alluded to

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.250
with my desertification slide,
and extreme weather events,

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:27.190
which also are going to face

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.990
resulting in internally displaced people,

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:34.700
and sometimes even
refugees across borders.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:35.790
That's the kind of definition.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:38.157
If you cross borders, you're a refugee.

00:18:38.157 --> 00:18:40.430
If you're displaced
within your own country,

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:43.040
you're an internally displaced person,

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.110
just for the vocabulary.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:49.490
Now I find this website to be fascinating,

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:51.240
the Alliance of Small Island States.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:54.090
This is 35 countries and five territories,

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:55.520
including all of ours.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:58.450
Members of the territories
of this organization

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:01.901
include Puerto Rico,
American Virgin Islands,

00:19:01.901 --> 00:19:06.690
and Northern Marianas Islands,
American Samoa, and Guam.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:09.110
Those are the five territories
in the United States,

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:10.760
and they're all members of this.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:15.490
So what they are worried
about is their existence.

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:17.810
Now take a look at what
two different modelers

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:22.160
using two different
strategies did in modeling

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:27.160
what will happen with one
meter ocean rise in Florida

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:32.430
in the next 30 years, and
next 50 years, I think it is.

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:36.670
And this is one modeler, and
the ecological devastation

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.247
that the entire Everglades
are going to be swamped

00:19:39.247 --> 00:19:42.390
with salt water, it can't be understated.

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:45.584
I mean, that'll be a dead zone.

00:19:45.584 --> 00:19:48.230
And then some very valuable real estate,

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:50.370
including all of the Kennedy Space Center

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:51.270
will be underwater.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:53.570
And this modeler, the one to the right

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:55.523
is much more pessimistic,

00:19:56.416 --> 00:20:01.416
much greater saturation of seawater

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:04.610
into the State of Florida.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:09.300
Now we will spend billions
and billions and billions

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.740
and hundreds maybe of billions of dollars

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:14.143
to build sea walls and mitigate this.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:20.670
So when we say we can't afford
to tackle climate change,

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:22.070
that's because people are thinking

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:24.520
in two, three-year economic windows.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:27.170
They're not thinking in 20,
30-year economic windows.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:31.610
Now, as temperatures rise,

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:34.440
more and more water vapor
evaporates into the atmosphere,

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:38.620
that is fuel for storms, as any
climatologist will tell you,

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.680
and the more heat and
water in the atmosphere

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.980
with warmer ocean surface temperatures

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:46.190
increases storm wind speeds,

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:50.150
and their analogous
temperature phenomena on land,

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:54.925
which increase the strength of tornadoes.

00:20:54.925 --> 00:20:59.925
So we can expect greenhouse gas emissions

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:04.180
and global warming to result
in more extreme weather events

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:05.480
and we've already seen it.

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:09.940
Now, keep in mind too,
that snow reflects heat,

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:13.800
it's white, reflects,
whereas rock absorbs it.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.750
Dark absorbs it, absorbs it.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.060
You learned that in grade school.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:22.460
And with fresh water snow
pack and glacier losses,

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:26.100
loss of snow pack, loss
of glaciers, earlier rain,

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:30.500
rain on snow, we are
going to see new cycles

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:31.840
of floods and then droughts.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:35.703
And I'll circle back to
that in a few slides.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:39.830
And the costs of these events are vast.

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:43.400
There are 15 Atlantic current hurricanes

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:48.400
that have cost at least
$10 billion each of damage.

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:52.480
And the king and queen of these hurricanes

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:57.480
were Katrina in New Orleans
and Harvey in Houston.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:04.550
Each of which resulted in
$125 billion worth of damage.

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:10.050
And it just is a miracle to
me that our political leaders

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:13.860
argue that we can't afford to mitigate

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:16.950
and adapt to climate change

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:21.180
when we're incurring these kinds of costs,

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:23.940
and we will be incurring vast new costs

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:28.920
for dubious technologies like seawalls,

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.580
that may or may not work.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.660
Now, vector-borne diseases,
this is sort of more my field.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:37.280
And in our school, we do a
lot of work in this space,

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:38.500
and that's a long list.

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:42.330
And I hate lecturers who give
you slides with long lists,

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:43.980
but I couldn't resist.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:45.100
Just to let you know

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:47.830
that you've got
mosquito-related infections,

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:49.550
'cause mosquitoes, remember,

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:54.550
if the mosquito is here and I'm malarious,

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:56.550
it has to bite me,

00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:01.530
then it has to live the sexual life course

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:05.630
of the malaria parasite,

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:10.500
which is now the male and
female are finding themselves,

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:15.500
and the sporozoites are gonna mature

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.600
after the oocysts mature in the midgut.

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.910
That takes eight to 12 days
depending upon the species.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.480
So the mosquito is not gonna infect Ann

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.560
if it doesn't live eight to 12 days.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:31.930
But if we help it live eight to 12 days

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:34.180
by being warmer and moisture,

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:37.700
then a greater proportion of
all the malarious mosquitoes

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.400
will survive to infect others.

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:43.080
So you can see how that
could have radical rise

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:45.920
in malarial incidents.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:48.220
Similarly, arboviruses and many

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:50.830
other vector-related infections

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:54.900
in which black flies like
warm moist environments,

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:57.220
so do snails, so do rodents,

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:00.920
so do tsetse flies and
reduviidae bugs, so do ticks,

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:05.630
and you can even have water that is warmer

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:06.700
for a longer period of time

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.760
that might harbor cholera
and other waterborne disease

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:12.560
for a longer time, so that's the story.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:17.560
And here's is Zimbabwe where 10 years ago,

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:22.650
those were the highlands, which
were largely malaria-free,

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:25.170
and 30 years from now,

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:29.040
there will be no malaria-free
zones of the nation.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:31.070
They will all be hyperendemic

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:33.960
or even holoendemic for malaria.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:38.600
Holoendemic being one
notch even more intense

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:40.750
in terms of transmission
than hyperendemic.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:46.730
Then we have population
pressures and shrinking land

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:52.050
and water assets that
also includes fish assets.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.740
Now this slide comes from Lake Victoria,

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.170
and you recall that that's a lake

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:03.770
that borders Uganda, Kenya, and Tanzania.

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:07.980
And if you look at 1960

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:11.760
and take a look at the population density,

00:25:11.760 --> 00:25:16.670
and then you go all the
way forward to 2015,

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:20.000
you'll see massive population growth

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:21.673
and pressures on the lake.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:26.660
And the fisher folk who
live around Lake Victoria

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:31.660
will make their living
supplying protein-rich fish

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:35.670
to the population, they
are now harvesting smaller

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:37.653
and smaller and smaller fish.

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:40.530
It's exactly what happened

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:43.710
in the Grand Banks off of Newfoundland,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:48.300
the richest cod fishing
zones in the entire world

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:51.400
that were fished out about 30 years ago

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:53.050
and have never rebounded,

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:56.480
the codfish have never
rebounded the Grand Banks,

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:59.790
which was mind-boggling to those of us

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.180
who remember that from our childhood,

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.013
where that was the
teeming source of codfish,

00:26:06.013 --> 00:26:09.480
and now you don't fish for
cod in the Grand Banks,

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:10.920
'cause there's no more cod.

00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:15.920
And the prospect of overfishing,
overgrazing on the land,

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:21.220
deforestation, farmland pressures

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:23.040
is almost certainly going to result

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:24.750
in hunger and climate refugees

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:29.350
in this very intensively
populated part of the world.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:30.430
And guess what?

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:35.410
We're repeating this
story in lake after lake

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.460
after lake ecosystem all around the world

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:40.060
where population pressures
are severely affecting.

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:44.030
Here's the Aral Sea and
the historic Aral Sea

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:46.400
is what all the blue represents.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:50.290
And the current is just this dark blue.

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:53.760
And you can see how the
historic was this vast area,

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:55.430
and the current is like this.

00:26:55.430 --> 00:27:00.078
That gentleman is standing
where the sea used to lap,

00:27:00.078 --> 00:27:03.880
where the sea used to reach.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:08.607
And there's a Navy worth of vessels

00:27:09.821 --> 00:27:11.440
that are completely abandoned,

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:14.930
because not only is there
much less water in Aral Sea,

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:18.713
but it's now saline water
for all practical purposes,

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.540
Great Salt Lake, kind of thing.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:26.350
And there are no fish in the
Aral Sea that can be harvested.

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:30.970
This is from crop growing
in which the rivers

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:35.970
were diverted to feed the very
water-thirsty cotton plants

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:43.650
and extensive use of
pesticides and fertilizers,

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:46.080
most of which end up up
in the Aral Sea eventually

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.450
poisoning the fish.

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:51.980
Now the exact same phenomenon
is happening in Lake Chad,

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:56.980
which is a fraction of its
size, Chad, Niger, Nigeria.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.010
It's estimated to be about half the size

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:03.070
that it has been historically.

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:07.370
And those of you who have
some California roots,

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:11.163
I went to high school and
college in California.

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:15.950
The Mono Lake used to be this vibrant,

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:19.980
large lake in Northern
California, it's a wasteland now,

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:24.980
because all that water was
used for urban water supplies,

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:29.623
and did not have adequate
restoration capacity.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:36.200
Now you can't mention
water, lakes, grazing

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.860
without mentioning forests,

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:44.670
because if you go to 10,000
years ago or 5,000 years ago,

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:49.670
more than half the habitable planet.

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.120
So 77% of the globe was habitable.

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:56.010
So get rid of Antarctica
and the Arctic, et cetera,

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.410
and get rid of the highest
mountains of the Himalayas

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:00.430
and look at the rest, that was 77%.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:03.810
So this represents 100% of the 77%

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:06.570
of which more than half was forest.

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:10.230
Even in 1700, more than half was forest.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.470
It fell below half by the
time we had a billion people

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:15.370
on Earth in 1900,

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:20.370
and you had 8% being used for
crops and 16% for grazing.

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:25.090
And that has doubled in a
little more than 100 years.

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:30.090
So now we're using 15% for
crops and 31% for grazing land.

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:33.120
And I don't know if this surprises you,

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:35.120
but it sure surprised me.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:37.508
If you'd given me a quiz and said,

00:29:37.508 --> 00:29:42.508
get this answer right or I'm
confiscating your favorite pet.

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:48.870
I would've bet on grazing, on crop land,

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:50.620
being more than grazing land,

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:52.470
but grazing land is more than double.

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:58.070
And a lot of deforestation is happening

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:00.503
to accommodate our thirst for meat,

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:05.263
our hunger for meat would
be a better term. (laughs)

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:08.770
And that is kind of what we're looking at

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:12.745
just in a 48-hour period.

00:30:12.745 --> 00:30:17.745
Those are the active fires
going on in Latin America.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:22.950
And you can see how the Amazon
is deforested and then burned

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:27.223
to prepare it for grazing and for crops.

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:34.760
Now I have offended people
in audiences with this slide,

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:40.903
it's from the UN, and I
wanna explain it clearly.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:46.820
So the United Nations estimates
that 222 million women

00:30:46.930 --> 00:30:49.540
who live in low and
middle income countries

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:52.420
would like to have
access to contraception,

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:54.053
but they do not have access.

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:59.560
That's what we mean by unmet
need for contraception.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:03.330
We're not talking about coercing anybody,

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.960
we're not even talking
about convincing anybody.

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:07.380
This is when you ask,

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.380
would you like to space your children

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:11.950
and not have a child every year?

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:14.540
Would you like to control the fertility

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.060
within your family structure?

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:17.550
And they say yes.

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:20.000
And do you have access to contraception

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:21.330
so you can control your fertility?

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:22.230
They say no.

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:25.003
Would you like it? Yes, that's unmet need.

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:29.960
If we could meet that unmet need,

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:33.279
that would handle demand in 50 million

00:31:33.279 --> 00:31:36.003
of persons under the age of 25.

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:39.903
14 million of them are
under the age of 20,

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:44.360
and 162 million of them

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:46.860
are in the world's poorest countries,

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:48.330
where you have the least capacity

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:49.813
to have a family of 10 or 12.

00:31:50.902 --> 00:31:54.970
That would prevent 63
million unwanted pregnancies,

00:31:54.970 --> 00:31:58.383
and it would prevent 19
million unsafe abortions.

00:31:59.953 --> 00:32:04.953
And it would also save 82,000
mothers' lives directly.

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:11.410
So education and sustainable
economic empowerment,

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:13.730
especially for girls and women does a lot

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.380
to advance control of fertility.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:19.870
We've seen that in study
after study internationally.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.300
There are exceptions where, for example,

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:25.963
you have very strong pro-natal sentiments,

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:32.980
Orthodox religions, for
example, of various sorts,

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:35.010
where there's a very strong desire

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:36.570
to have very large families.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:41.440
So then the education and
economic empowerment formula

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:42.273
doesn't quite work.

00:32:42.273 --> 00:32:45.110
But in every other environment,
it works very well.

00:32:45.110 --> 00:32:47.400
So there's a vast unmet need.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:51.400
And keep in mind that
when I was born in 1954,

00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:53.760
there were 2.7 billion people on Earth,

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:56.753
and now there are 7.9
billion people on Earth.

00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:03.720
And it took us a 100 years

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:07.040
to double the population previously,

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:09.240
and it's taken us half of 100 years

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.540
to increase it almost threefold.

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:15.540
So we either take global
population control seriously,

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:17.400
or we don't.

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:19.760
We don't take it seriously,

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:24.760
then you're gonna have a
lot of underfed children

00:33:24.898 --> 00:33:29.490
in families that are
not able to support six,

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:31.570
seven, eight, nine, 10 children,

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:34.980
and didn't want that number of children.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:38.410
There's a lot of individual
and community tragedy,

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:42.770
but there's also an existential
threat to Planet Earth,

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:46.570
because reasonable people have argued

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:51.570
that 7.9 billion people is too many people

00:33:52.540 --> 00:33:54.940
for a good quality of
life for people on Earth.

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:59.593
And we can do a lot if all
we do is meet unmet need.

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:03.680
So I dwelled on this slide

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.023
to make sure you understand
my point of view.

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:09.560
I am not looking to coerce anybody,

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:12.320
no one child policy, no nothing.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:17.010
Just meet the need of women
who want contraception,

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:19.170
meet that need, and we will do a lot

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:22.620
to turn to the right direction.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:24.720
So let's take a look at events

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:28.640
in the past two years, 2020, 2021.

00:34:28.640 --> 00:34:31.770
We had Australian and
Western USA wildfires

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.100
that were the worst in the history

00:34:33.100 --> 00:34:35.450
of those two countries by far,

00:34:35.450 --> 00:34:37.433
the largest, the most intense.

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:40.070
We had tremendous challenges

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:43.270
in smoke exposure and poor air quality,

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:45.800
and we had a lot of mental health impacts.

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:49.300
People who lost everything,
lost their homes,

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:51.743
some people lost lives of loved ones,

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:56.130
firefighters were lost, on and on.

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:58.560
I've already mentioned
the temperature records

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.490
of the past eight years
being the hottest ever,

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:06.490
but they're also local venue
hottest day ever records

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:10.927
that have been broken in
historic sweep from Siberia,

00:35:14.780 --> 00:35:17.393
all the way to Arizona and Florida,

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:21.200
places that had broke their records

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:25.930
by five degrees Fahrenheit,
10 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:35:25.930 --> 00:35:27.430
There was one place in Siberia

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:29.780
that broke the record by
40 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:35:31.750 --> 00:35:36.310
And 2020 and 2016 were the hottest years

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:37.230
in recorded history,

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:40.960
although 2020 isn't that
different from 2021.

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:44.653
In fact, they were judged to
be virtually indistinguishable.

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:48.440
COVID had an interesting result,

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:53.440
which was a favorable impact
on greenhouse gas emissions

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:57.010
because there was less driving,

00:35:57.010 --> 00:35:58.910
there was less industry, and the like.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:05.210
But the impact on food supply
chains were very complex.

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:07.430
Some places were not severely impacted,

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:09.130
other places were severely impacted.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:14.060
So I can't exactly cheer for
COVID having done as much good

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:16.670
because a lot of these are now rebounding

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:21.060
to his historic levels of
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:23.360
Finally, we'll talk about evolving drought

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:26.070
and flood cycles and famine risk.

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:31.070
So this is a map where
they are showing the size

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:36.123
of glacial regions in the Himalaya.

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:42.450
And the loss in glacial
size is represented

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:45.980
by the red color.

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:48.000
If it's white color, no change,

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:50.458
and if it's blue, a
little bit of addition,

00:36:50.458 --> 00:36:55.458
so overwhelmingly loss,
especially in the glaciers

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:57.590
that are closest to population,

00:36:57.590 --> 00:37:02.590
areas that are feeding the Indus River,

00:37:03.160 --> 00:37:08.160
the Brahmaputra, Ganges River,
and the Mekong River deltas.

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:12.830
Now 1.5 billion people
live in these deltas.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:16.178
And what is now happening is early,

00:37:16.178 --> 00:37:20.453
three-week shorter snow pack,

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:25.220
and earlier melting,

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:29.343
and earlier springtime flooding,

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:33.363
and later summer drought.

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:38.220
So it's not actually a
net decline or increase

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:40.845
in total precipitation.

00:37:40.845 --> 00:37:43.780
It's more rain, less snow,

00:37:43.780 --> 00:37:48.220
and earlier snow pack
melt and glacial melt

00:37:48.220 --> 00:37:51.720
with greater volumes in
the rivers early in season,

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:54.700
and then drought volumes
later in the season.

00:37:54.700 --> 00:37:56.910
And none of this is good news.

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:01.850
None of this suggests that
we are going to have farmers

00:38:01.850 --> 00:38:04.970
and villagers better off
with these conditions

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:06.260
than they've been historically.

00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:07.860
They're going to be worse off

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:10.593
with early floods, late droughts.

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:15.650
Here is Borneo over
about a 19-year period.

00:38:15.650 --> 00:38:19.060
And if it's green, it means forest,

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:22.870
and if it's orange, it means no forest.

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:24.860
And to the right, you
have Northeast Brazil.

00:38:24.860 --> 00:38:27.660
If it's blue green, it's forest loss,

00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:28.870
and if it's red, there's forest,

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:32.760
different kind of infrared photography.

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:36.940
And to the left, it's
because of deforestation

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:40.110
and population pressures, grazing,

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:44.833
and a little bit of oil exploration,

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:49.910
not a little bit, in
certain areas, a lot of it,

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:52.360
and to the right, it's all mining.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:55.380
Of course, I could have
taken a different picture

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:56.950
from Brazil in the Amazon.

00:38:56.950 --> 00:38:58.870
It would've been due to deforestation.

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:01.750
So there is a lot going on in our world

00:39:01.750 --> 00:39:02.950
that is not favorable.

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:04.143
Now the good news.

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:07.650
With research, policy, education, action,

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:10.870
this is both feasible and necessary.

00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:14.530
We can confront global climate
change at the same time,

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:18.670
take a look at issues
of loss of biodiversity

00:39:18.670 --> 00:39:22.230
on our planet and human overpopulation.

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:26.180
So efficient transportation
looms very large.

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:31.180
We are burning fossil fuels

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:34.940
when we could be using solar power.

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:38.670
So three years ago, my wife
and I went solar in our house,

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:40.430
cost us $33,000.

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:43.520
We got about 10,000 back
from our taxes and the feds,

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:46.150
and we got about 3,000
back from the state,

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:48.580
so that was a net of $20,000.

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:52.680
We pay virtually no, just the hookup fee,

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:54.180
and we bought an electric car,

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:59.180
so our electric car is
powered by our solar panels.

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:04.050
And are we gonna change the
history of Planet Earth?

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:06.360
By no means, but I'm hoping

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:08.470
we might influence our neighbors.

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:10.260
And I'm hoping you might do the same

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:11.390
and might influence your neighbors.

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:15.480
In other words, there has to
be some level of tipping point

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:18.240
where some of us get ahead of the curve

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.283
and take a risk on new technology,

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:26.850
'cause it's not new anymore.

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:29.730
Solar panels prices have plummeted,

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:32.070
and it really is very favorable

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:34.973
if your home happens
to be properly suited.

00:40:35.916 --> 00:40:38.470
Some people live in the
middle of the forest

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:41.070
and short of cutting down a
bunch of trees, it won't work.

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:46.070
But many of us have some solar
exposure and we can go solar.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:50.060
So that's at the micro level,
but at the macro level,

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:51.720
you can do what Bishop's farms.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:53.270
How many of you have had a pie

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.120
from Bishop's out in Guilford?

00:40:55.120 --> 00:40:57.750
They are completely carbon neutral.

00:40:57.750 --> 00:40:59.660
That's not because they're
buying car carbon credits,

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:01.370
they've become carbon neutral

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:04.953
because they've deployed a
number of their orchards,

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:11.950
and they have turned
them into solar farms.

00:41:11.950 --> 00:41:15.770
So all of the energy utilization of,

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:20.770
yeah, I think they have some gas vehicles,

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.280
but they're selling enough,

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:26.750
they're installing enough energy,

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:29.343
solar energy into the
grid to balance that.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:33.883
We can do so much more here at Yale.

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:39.140
My building, the LEPH
building, many of you know,

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:44.140
60 colleges is, out of
150 buildings on campus

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:51.220
that had energy audits was the
15th least energy efficient.

00:41:54.100 --> 00:41:56.920
I'm ashamed to walk into that
building every single day.

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:59.250
And they've done some mitigation,

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:01.287
remediation, I should say,

00:42:01.287 --> 00:42:03.070
and I think were not quite that bad,

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:06.440
but they haven't done
the definitive things.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:09.280
And we can power electric
cars, buses, trains.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:12.080
There's nothing stopping us,
now we have the technology.

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:15.850
Energy conservation,
the notion of having it

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:19.750
at 68 in the winter and 72 in the summer

00:42:19.750 --> 00:42:22.683
is not that big a sacrifice,
but people don't do it.

00:42:23.530 --> 00:42:26.060
And it just enrages me when
I go into a public space

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:29.540
in the summer, and I'm
sorry, I forgot my sweater,

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:32.910
'cause we just keep it, the
Europeans think we're nuts

00:42:32.910 --> 00:42:34.697
because they don't do that.

00:42:34.697 --> 00:42:37.270
And you go into a hotel in Europe

00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:38.103
in the middle of the winter

00:42:38.103 --> 00:42:40.363
and you better have a sweater
'cause it's a little chilly.

00:42:41.702 --> 00:42:44.290
And you go in the summer, it's
gonna be a little bit warmer

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:48.960
maybe than you're used to,
but we just have to adapt.

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:50.870
We're just wasting energy.

00:42:50.870 --> 00:42:53.130
We have double the energy utilization,

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:56.122
the average American than
the average European.

00:42:56.122 --> 00:42:59.700
We have automatic shut
offs, we have LEED buildings

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:02.670
that we ought to be promulgating.

00:43:02.670 --> 00:43:05.510
We have many other
sustainable energy sources

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:08.040
and I put nuclear in yellow

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:11.573
just to stimulate conversation later.

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:16.370
And economic incentives, cap
and trade, carbon credits,

00:43:16.370 --> 00:43:19.560
tax credits, and the whole
ocean of sustainable land use

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:21.410
and population stabilization.

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:24.060
Do we really have to eat
as much meat as we do?

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:28.220
Do we really have to as much
grazing land as we have?

00:43:28.220 --> 00:43:30.660
Couldn't we take advantage of the fact

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:35.420
that it takes 10 kilos of plant protein

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:38.430
to grow one kilo of meat protein?

00:43:38.430 --> 00:43:40.980
Couldn't we eat some of
that plant protein instead?

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:43.850
The answer is of course.

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:46.580
Now political change and
commitment is essential

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:51.580
and we need better leadership.

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:54.468
There was a moratorium and
fossil fuel exploration

00:43:54.468 --> 00:43:58.520
with massive shift
alternative energy sources.

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:00.860
How many of you noticed
the announcement last week

00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:03.030
from the Biden administration?

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:06.610
Opening up hundreds of
thousands of acres of land

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:09.883
for oil exploration again.

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:16.940
And, that I call the
Ukraine policy set-back.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.250
And I put that in yellow 'cause
that might be of interest

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.040
to us to discuss in a moment.

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:24.020
Commitment to conservation
with policy shifts,

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:25.130
as I mentioned.

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:28.970
We've had tremendous success
changing tobacco attitudes,

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:32.803
changing drinking under
the influence attitudes.

00:44:36.244 --> 00:44:38.230
You don't smoke in airplanes anymore,

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:39.920
you don't smoke in buildings anymore.

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:43.500
And we've seen, and we have
much higher tobacco taxes

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:45.530
and we've seen tobacco use drop.

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:48.573
We can create, we can work
with our economist friends,

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:53.280
and we can incentives to make
it more expensive for you

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:56.760
to pollute and less expensive
for you not to pollute.

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:59.000
And if we don't do it, we're in trouble.

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:02.390
We need aggressive
preservation of wild places

00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:05.920
to enhance biodiversity
and mitigate CO2 release,

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:09.210
reforestation, control
pesticide use and pollution.

00:45:09.210 --> 00:45:11.530
And there's a lot of
good advice coming out

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:12.680
of the Glasgow conference.

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:16.193
I'm proud to say Yale, colleague of ours,

00:45:19.320 --> 00:45:23.570
Secretary Kerry, is spearheading this now

00:45:23.570 --> 00:45:26.060
as the international climate change

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:28.130
at the cabinet level, I might add,

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:30.860
and we couldn't ask for a better voice.

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:33.350
Now the emergency response and planning,

00:45:33.350 --> 00:45:35.830
this is something that's
in our bailiwick as nurses,

00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:38.690
as planners, public health folks.

00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:40.690
We need to anticipate heat waves,

00:45:40.690 --> 00:45:45.140
drought and extreme weather
modeling, forecasting,

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:48.451
relocation of persons doing
the flooding and storm.

00:45:48.451 --> 00:45:53.451
We can plan for the unknown knowns.

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:57.140
We know it's gonna happen,
we don't know when,

00:45:57.140 --> 00:45:59.410
but we can plan for it
so that when it happens,

00:45:59.410 --> 00:46:00.860
we are ready.

00:46:00.860 --> 00:46:05.460
What about if we had taken
the advice of the CDC in 2017

00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:07.370
when they came out with updates

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:11.210
of their pandemic
influenza prevention plans?

00:46:11.210 --> 00:46:14.500
Which was like a roadmap
what we should be doing

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:17.757
to get ready for COVID, we
just didn't know it was COVID.

00:46:17.757 --> 00:46:21.000
It was largely ignored and
largely sat on a shelf,

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:23.620
and then we had COVID
and we weren't ready.

00:46:23.620 --> 00:46:25.913
We knew what to do, we just weren't ready.

00:46:26.890 --> 00:46:30.730
Incentivize and improve the
appeal of vegetable-based diets.

00:46:30.730 --> 00:46:35.303
The head of Yale hospitality
here is incredibly creative.

00:46:36.624 --> 00:46:38.560
He and his experts have lopped off

00:46:38.560 --> 00:46:42.830
half of the meat consumption
of Yale undergraduates

00:46:42.830 --> 00:46:45.420
over the last five years with nary a peep

00:46:47.090 --> 00:46:52.090
through creative use of
vegetables, creative menus,

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:00.410
and strategies for making foods appealing

00:47:00.410 --> 00:47:02.320
that are largely plant-driven.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:04.320
If he can do it, we can
do it in our households

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:06.943
and we can do it in our
institutional settings.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:11.500
And control of air and water
pollution is a no-brainer.

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:14.380
Vector control, bednets,
community engagement.

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:17.340
That's a big one 'cause
we've got to educate.

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:20.390
Water and hygiene innovation,
the old drip technologies

00:47:20.390 --> 00:47:22.770
that the Israelis taught us 60 years ago

00:47:23.980 --> 00:47:27.019
in the kibbutzes, protect the environment,

00:47:27.019 --> 00:47:32.019
retain ecological diversity,
and the contraception agenda.

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:37.560
So the kids know we've gotta
do something, Greta Thunberg.

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:42.340
The South Africans have
faced this in a big way.

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:44.890
There's my favorite Bishop's Orchard,

00:47:44.890 --> 00:47:48.737
and here are divestment protestors saying,

00:47:48.737 --> 00:47:51.450
"Yeah, Yale with your
$44 billion endowment,

00:47:51.450 --> 00:47:54.500
do you really have to be
owning fossil fuel companies?

00:47:54.500 --> 00:47:57.997
Really? Can't you be a
role model for others?"

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:01.150
And this I wanna end with,

00:48:01.150 --> 00:48:04.490
habitat loss is a major
threat to biodiversity.

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:06.830
I've kind of gone over my time, I think.

00:48:06.830 --> 00:48:08.810
Am I right? I'm okay?

00:48:08.810 --> 00:48:10.350
Okay, well, that's a miracle.

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:14.880
So habitat degradation, exploitation,

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:18.310
invasive species,
pollution, climate change.

00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:20.970
Those are what are
affecting fish, mammals,

00:48:20.970 --> 00:48:24.000
reptiles, and amphibians, and birds now,

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:26.690
and you can just expect
the climate change blue

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:28.780
to grow and grow and grow.

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:31.580
So this is kind of 10 years ago

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:34.330
and it's just gonna get worse
on the climate change side.

00:48:34.330 --> 00:48:37.340
So we've already had tremendous threats

00:48:37.340 --> 00:48:41.150
to habitats and biodiversity.

00:48:41.150 --> 00:48:44.129
It's estimated we've
lost three billion birds

00:48:44.129 --> 00:48:47.370
in North America in recent years.

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:50.683
And insect populations
have plummeted as well.

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:54.650
And you might say, "Well, that's
great. Well, great by me."

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:58.480
I don't like bugs, but that's
what the amphibians eat,

00:48:58.480 --> 00:48:59.560
that's what the birds eat.

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:02.810
That's a vital part of our food chain,

00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:06.610
and why are we losing insects?

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:08.250
What are we doing to the planet

00:49:08.250 --> 00:49:10.070
that's resulting in the loss of insects?

00:49:10.070 --> 00:49:13.330
That's an even more compelling issue.

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:15.963
So we are very focused on education.

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:19.405
I was so proud when the very first year

00:49:19.405 --> 00:49:23.590
that we launched our climate change

00:49:23.590 --> 00:49:27.570
and health certificate
program, we had three nurses.

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:30.803
They were all from Mass General.

00:49:31.930 --> 00:49:35.330
And I asked them when
I had an opportunity.

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:37.100
Why are you taking this course?

00:49:37.100 --> 00:49:39.430
They said, "Well, we are the three people

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:41.340
who care the most about climate change

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:43.170
in the nursing school at Harvard.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:44.880
We all happen to be at Mass General,

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:46.220
we're all helping each other.

00:49:46.220 --> 00:49:48.320
We're going to take your curriculum

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:51.400
and we're going to work it
into the nursing curriculum

00:49:51.400 --> 00:49:52.670
of Harvard University."

00:49:52.670 --> 00:49:54.550
And I just was the happiest man

00:49:55.440 --> 00:49:58.400
because to me that was the whole point

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:00.510
of the climate change certificate.

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:04.980
And now we have it as a MOOC, Mass Online,

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:07.499
something, what is MOOC stand for?

00:50:07.499 --> 00:50:12.499
Course, somebody knows, Mass
Online something course,

00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:18.660
Open Course, open, Mass
Online Open Course.

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:22.820
And I can't remember if it's
free or if it's 49 bucks,

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:25.030
but it's incredibly cheap

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:26.680
and that's pretty much everything here

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:29.010
without the love and attention.

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.630
And we have our new climate change

00:50:31.630 --> 00:50:35.020
and health concentration in our MPH.

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:38.160
We have an emphasis in
our executive online MPH.

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:43.030
So we even have people who
live in Milford and Madison

00:50:43.030 --> 00:50:43.863
who are taking this

00:50:43.863 --> 00:50:44.900
just 'cause it's so much easier

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:46.450
when they're working professionals.

00:50:46.450 --> 00:50:47.960
Then we got people in California,

00:50:47.960 --> 00:50:49.880
and Louisiana, and elsewhere.

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:52.880
And then we're doing K-12 outreach

00:50:52.880 --> 00:50:54.720
for education in our schools.

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:59.530
A lot of community outreach
talks to all sorts of audiences.

00:50:59.530 --> 00:51:04.460
I gave a talk to the
Connecticut arborists,

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:08.610
the people who chopped down
trees and replant them.

00:51:08.610 --> 00:51:11.420
And somebody had heard me talk

00:51:11.420 --> 00:51:12.450
and they asked me to give a talk,

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:16.499
so I slept up to West Harvard
and met the arborists.

00:51:16.499 --> 00:51:19.130
Anybody else who wants to hear about this?

00:51:19.130 --> 00:51:22.460
Now you happen to be in
an epicenter of expertise

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:24.223
right here in this building.

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:28.530
You can read about it on your own website.

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:31.700
And you spearheaded this Graduate Forum

00:51:31.700 --> 00:51:34.780
and Global Youth Summit just last year.

00:51:34.780 --> 00:51:38.520
Dean Curth co-chairs the
Sustainability Committee.

00:51:38.520 --> 00:51:40.590
She's one of three deans on that committee

00:51:40.590 --> 00:51:44.290
and she's a leader, Yale and
nationwide in this space.

00:51:44.290 --> 00:51:45.240
And I'm not kidding.

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:49.450
She literally educated the
president of our university,

00:51:49.450 --> 00:51:52.010
and that's why planetary health

00:51:52.010 --> 00:51:54.250
is one of our major priorities.

00:51:54.250 --> 00:51:56.023
It was deemed a major priority

00:51:56.023 --> 00:52:01.023
by Scott Strobel's old science committee,

00:52:01.860 --> 00:52:05.770
but it was not well-packaged,
it was not well-presented,

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:08.350
and Ann fixed all of that
almost single handedly.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:10.480
And then Associate Dean Nelson

00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:13.060
spearheads Planetary Health
in its global context

00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:17.470
being the associate dean for global health

00:52:17.470 --> 00:52:19.570
and planetary health, something like that.

00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:22.870
I wanted to mention Jodi Sherman

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:23.810
and the work she's doing

00:52:23.810 --> 00:52:26.650
on Healthcare Environmental
Sustainability.

00:52:26.650 --> 00:52:28.840
Those of you who are deeply engaged

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:31.320
in hospital care, which
is a number of you.

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:34.040
This is very much focused on the fact

00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:37.340
that if the hospitals and
healthcare environments

00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:38.750
of the world were a country,

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:43.363
they would be the 11th largest
greenhouse gas emitter.

00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:47.690
So the healthcare industry itself

00:52:47.690 --> 00:52:49.250
is one of the worst offenders

00:52:49.250 --> 00:52:51.193
in terms of greenhouse gas emission,

00:52:52.180 --> 00:52:55.940
and that's because we use
it once and throw it away.

00:52:55.940 --> 00:52:59.180
That's because we have
our anesthetic gases,

00:52:59.180 --> 00:53:02.820
which we're pumping and
only 3% is metabolized,

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:06.810
and 97% is re-released,
and on and on and on,

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:08.671
all sorts of reasons.

00:53:08.671 --> 00:53:10.300
You wanna get active, call Jodi.

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:14.490
She is a force of nature
and doing very fine work

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:15.930
out of the School of Public Health

00:53:15.930 --> 00:53:19.100
in the medical school and
working also with Ann.

00:53:19.100 --> 00:53:21.040
And many other nursing resources.

00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:24.000
Yesterday or day before,
I did a PubMed search,

00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:28.480
I found 775, just for
climate change and nursing,

00:53:28.480 --> 00:53:31.030
that's all I did, simplest
search in the world,

00:53:31.030 --> 00:53:34.393
and nearly half of them were
from the last three years.

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:38.510
And I found a couple from Ann

00:53:38.510 --> 00:53:41.910
in the Journal of Nursing
Scholarship, two of them.

00:53:41.910 --> 00:53:44.980
And I also found this collaboration

00:53:44.980 --> 00:53:48.240
of the Alliance of Nurses
for Healthy Environments

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:50.370
and it's Nurses Climate Challenge.

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.170
I accidentally put an apostrophe.

00:53:52.170 --> 00:53:53.350
They don't have an apostrophe.

00:53:53.350 --> 00:53:55.850
Nurses Climate Challenge Initiative,

00:53:55.850 --> 00:53:57.810
collaborating with Health Without Harm,

00:53:57.810 --> 00:53:59.750
and these are a couple of resources there.

00:53:59.750 --> 00:54:03.290
So nurses are at the forefront

00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:06.450
of the demand that we do
something at about this,

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:09.870
and I think public health people are too.

00:54:09.870 --> 00:54:12.190
I'd like to get our
medical schools on board,

00:54:12.190 --> 00:54:13.850
I'd like to get our universities on board,

00:54:13.850 --> 00:54:15.260
and we can actually make a difference.

00:54:15.260 --> 00:54:17.170
So thank you for your attention.

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:21.719
A few sites from our site
and looking forward to tea.

00:54:21.719 --> 00:54:23.600
(audience applauding)

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:27.750
So who wants to talk about
Ukraine, or nuclear power,

00:54:27.750 --> 00:54:29.650
or any other topic?

00:54:29.650 --> 00:54:31.463
Any questions or comments, please?

00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:35.353
Yes?

00:54:35.353 --> 00:54:36.595
- [Audience Member] I wanna
know about this nuclear power.

00:54:36.595 --> 00:54:39.540
- Okay, so I'm an old-fashioned guy

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:41.110
and I saw the China Syndrome

00:54:41.110 --> 00:54:44.160
and I lived through Three
Mile Island and Chernobyl.

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:45.020
And if you asked me,

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:47.030
do I wanna live next to
a nuclear power plant?

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:49.110
I'd say, "No, thank you."

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:54.110
On the other hand, nuclear
power is very benign

00:54:54.250 --> 00:54:57.370
from a greenhouse gas
emissions point of view.

00:54:57.370 --> 00:55:00.020
You produce a vast amount of energy

00:55:00.020 --> 00:55:01.850
with no greenhouse gas emissions.

00:55:01.850 --> 00:55:03.610
So I'm not saying that the process

00:55:03.610 --> 00:55:05.280
doesn't have some
greenhouse gas emissions,

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:07.640
but it's tiny, tiny fraction.

00:55:07.640 --> 00:55:10.690
And you generate vast amounts of energy.

00:55:10.690 --> 00:55:13.750
One massive problem, well, two problems.

00:55:13.750 --> 00:55:17.913
One is safety, Three Mile
Island, Chernobyl, Fukushima.

00:55:18.910 --> 00:55:23.047
But the other is, where do you
put the spent nuclear fuel,

00:55:25.450 --> 00:55:26.840
which still is radioactive

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:29.480
for the next 30,000 years or whatever?

00:55:29.480 --> 00:55:32.920
And we thought we had a
plan for Yucca Mountain,

00:55:32.920 --> 00:55:35.880
which was vetted for 50 years

00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:40.880
as a highly stable mountain,
seismically inactive,

00:55:42.430 --> 00:55:47.430
a natural depth, and a place
that you literally could store.

00:55:48.590 --> 00:55:53.590
And the Nevada senators
and congresspersons

00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:00.670
were too powerful, and they put a kibosh,

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:04.540
and President Obama abandoned
the Yucca Mountain plan.

00:56:04.540 --> 00:56:07.770
And now we're storing
it all over the place.

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:10.190
San Onofre in California,

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:12.240
not far from where I went to high school,

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:18.533
is storing all of their nuclear
waste right there on site,

00:56:19.580 --> 00:56:21.870
and they've walled it
off and they've got it.

00:56:21.870 --> 00:56:25.040
But it's sitting there
near the Pacific Ocean,

00:56:25.040 --> 00:56:27.127
and all these experts are saying,

00:56:27.127 --> 00:56:30.180
"Oh, it can stand the big
one, the the big quake."

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:31.420
I don't believe that for a second.

00:56:31.420 --> 00:56:33.290
So they're storing it there

00:56:33.290 --> 00:56:35.160
'cause they have nowhere to take it.

00:56:35.160 --> 00:56:38.050
So if we were serious about nuclear,

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:41.560
we would have to get
very serious about issues

00:56:41.560 --> 00:56:45.810
like if you're gonna put this near water

00:56:45.810 --> 00:56:47.810
because you have to have the cooling,

00:56:47.810 --> 00:56:49.800
so you have to put these near water

00:56:49.800 --> 00:56:51.770
or you have to have water available,

00:56:51.770 --> 00:56:54.400
then you better think about a tsunami,

00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:59.100
and you better think about the
big earthquake in California.

00:56:59.100 --> 00:57:03.230
You better think about all
of the safety considerations.

00:57:03.230 --> 00:57:05.920
So we need a coherent safety approach

00:57:05.920 --> 00:57:10.920
and we need a coherent
nuclear disposal approach.

00:57:10.940 --> 00:57:12.790
But I have changed my mind over the years.

00:57:12.790 --> 00:57:17.323
I think that the urgency of
the global warming crisis

00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:20.780
has to make us rethink nuclear.

00:57:20.780 --> 00:57:23.218
Now I'd much prefer that we
reduce our energy consumption,

00:57:23.218 --> 00:57:26.680
I'd much prefer for that
we go solar and electric,

00:57:26.680 --> 00:57:28.380
I'd much prefer many other things,

00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:30.600
but I think we're gonna need nuclear too.

00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:32.660
Now the French have shown
it can be done safely.

00:57:32.660 --> 00:57:34.999
The French have had the highest proportion

00:57:34.999 --> 00:57:37.800
of nuclear power in their grid

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:40.140
of any country in the Western world,

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:43.740
or I think any country anywhere,

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:47.450
and they have ever had a nuclear accident.

00:57:47.450 --> 00:57:52.450
But their nuclear waste
storage is still suboptimal.

00:57:52.700 --> 00:57:55.100
So that's what I personally
think about nuclear.

00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:00.830
Yes, please.

00:58:00.830 --> 00:58:02.070
- [Audience Member] You
talked a little bit earlier

00:58:02.070 --> 00:58:04.830
about the attitude of short-term planning

00:58:04.830 --> 00:58:06.680
versus long term planning.

00:58:06.680 --> 00:58:08.830
And I wondered if you had any examples

00:58:08.830 --> 00:58:11.760
of whether that be
domestically or abroad people

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:14.080
who are doing a good job of saying like,

00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:16.027
okay, we need to look at the 40-year plan,

00:58:16.027 --> 00:58:17.670
the 50-year plan, as opposed

00:58:17.670 --> 00:58:21.350
to the four-year political
elected office term

00:58:21.350 --> 00:58:22.650
and how to kind of bring that together.

00:58:22.650 --> 00:58:24.170
Is anybody doing a good job with that

00:58:24.170 --> 00:58:26.387
that we could model?

00:58:26.387 --> 00:58:30.020
- Well, I might pluck out the Danes.

00:58:30.020 --> 00:58:34.330
So the Danes figured there
was a big problem 30 years ago

00:58:34.330 --> 00:58:37.950
and they invested massively
in a resource that they have,

00:58:37.950 --> 00:58:39.290
a lot of other people don't have,

00:58:39.290 --> 00:58:41.693
which is known as the North Sea wind.

00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:47.100
And I think the second
largest wind farm in the world

00:58:47.100 --> 00:58:50.680
is off the coast of
Denmark, and it's in a part

00:58:50.680 --> 00:58:54.610
where it's out literally in the North Sea,

00:58:54.610 --> 00:58:58.150
not where they're in
intense bird populations,

00:58:58.150 --> 00:59:01.890
and they figured out the technology

00:59:01.890 --> 00:59:06.890
of how to seat the wind farm into the sea

00:59:07.320 --> 00:59:09.550
and how to harvest the energy

00:59:09.550 --> 00:59:13.890
by bringing it subterranean back to land.

00:59:13.890 --> 00:59:17.410
And I wish I could remember the proportion

00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:20.670
of all the energy grid in
Denmark that's from wind,

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:24.010
but it's a really high
number, like 30% or something.

00:59:24.010 --> 00:59:26.100
And this is a test of concept.

00:59:26.100 --> 00:59:28.290
They proved it could be done.

00:59:28.290 --> 00:59:32.630
I have been to Xinjiang province,

00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:35.880
the province with a
majority Uyghur population

00:59:35.880 --> 00:59:37.630
in Northeast China.

00:59:37.630 --> 00:59:41.850
Now there is tremendous
political disruption,

00:59:41.850 --> 00:59:45.240
and discrimination, and harm
being done to the Uyghurs

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:46.510
in that part of China.

00:59:46.510 --> 00:59:49.210
But before that, I had a project there

00:59:49.210 --> 00:59:51.600
and I went through the largest wind tunnel

00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:54.110
and this was between two mountain chains.

00:59:54.110 --> 00:59:56.340
They took advantage of the natural wind

00:59:56.340 --> 01:00:01.340
that was feeding this big
valley and they built wind farm

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:04.623
that was further than the eye could see,

01:00:06.050 --> 01:00:08.690
all with modern wind technology.

01:00:08.690 --> 01:00:11.270
So there's a lot that
can be done with wind.

01:00:11.270 --> 01:00:15.323
Solar technology has improved massively,

01:00:16.219 --> 01:00:19.050
and we have some examples
in our own country

01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:23.930
of highly successful solar strategies.

01:00:23.930 --> 01:00:26.550
But I think where I'm most disturbed

01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:30.900
is when I go visit my younger
son who lives in Orlando,

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.180
and I just see so few solar panels.

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:36.540
And this is like, you think about Florida,

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:39.470
you think about Arizona, and then think

01:00:39.470 --> 01:00:44.210
about Chad and Niger, and Mali,

01:00:44.210 --> 01:00:46.410
and some of the poorest
countries of the world,

01:00:46.410 --> 01:00:50.860
and think about all the wasted energy

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:55.860
by not harvesting the sun in
these sun-intensive venues.

01:00:56.870 --> 01:01:01.220
And why can't we have a global campaign,

01:01:01.220 --> 01:01:03.690
not to mention COVID vaccine,

01:01:03.690 --> 01:01:08.690
but to mention solar
panels in high sun zones.

01:01:09.960 --> 01:01:14.430
This is cherry picking

01:01:14.430 --> 01:01:17.000
something that would be
relatively straightforward,

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:20.720
'cause fewer solar panels
would be more efficient

01:01:20.720 --> 01:01:22.820
in high sun regions.

01:01:22.820 --> 01:01:25.330
But even in low sun regions,
even in a cloudy day,

01:01:25.330 --> 01:01:27.973
you can harvest energy from a solar panel.

01:01:30.510 --> 01:01:32.240
Now I was personally disappointed

01:01:32.240 --> 01:01:34.640
with the Biden administration decision

01:01:34.640 --> 01:01:38.360
because I'm at the point in
my awareness of this problem

01:01:38.360 --> 01:01:43.360
that I feel that we can't be
compromising at every turn.

01:01:45.570 --> 01:01:47.210
If we say that federal lands

01:01:47.210 --> 01:01:50.320
are not gonna be used
for oil exploitation,

01:01:50.320 --> 01:01:51.403
then we stick to it.

01:01:52.250 --> 01:01:55.743
And if we have a problem
with gasoline prices

01:01:57.720 --> 01:01:59.360
being too high,

01:01:59.360 --> 01:02:03.487
we look the people in the eye
on television, and we say,

01:02:03.487 --> 01:02:06.700
"Do you have a bus that goes

01:02:06.700 --> 01:02:10.080
from your home home to
your work or a train?

01:02:10.080 --> 01:02:11.740
Could you call a neighbor?

01:02:11.740 --> 01:02:16.740
Can you cut your gasoline
prices by carpooling?

01:02:18.780 --> 01:02:23.430
What can you do to help the
Ukrainian people at this time

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:27.370
without sacrificing our
grandchildren's future

01:02:27.370 --> 01:02:29.530
in a warm world?

01:02:29.530 --> 01:02:32.140
And why can't we call
on the American people

01:02:32.140 --> 01:02:36.780
for a war time response and draw on us

01:02:36.780 --> 01:02:38.970
for inconvenience ourselves

01:02:38.970 --> 01:02:43.970
and reducing our dependence
on oil by conservation?"

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:46.270
That's what I would've liked
to hear from the White House,

01:02:46.270 --> 01:02:51.270
not, "Okay, we'll open up
180,000 acres of land,"

01:02:52.606 --> 01:02:55.950
I forget how much it was,
"for oil exploitation,"

01:02:55.950 --> 01:02:57.560
which we're not even gonna get that oil

01:02:57.560 --> 01:02:59.210
for two, three years.

01:02:59.210 --> 01:03:03.263
So to me, that was a very
cynical bid to win votes.

01:03:05.170 --> 01:03:07.532
I hate to say it, that's what I think.

01:03:07.532 --> 01:03:09.482
- [Ann] Always says what he thinks.

01:03:09.482 --> 01:03:11.180
(indistinct)

01:03:11.180 --> 01:03:13.330
- [Audience Member] Had a question.

01:03:13.330 --> 01:03:16.599
While you made some of the resolutions

01:03:16.599 --> 01:03:21.280
could be moving more
towards electric costs.

01:03:21.280 --> 01:03:23.207
And my question (indistinct),

01:03:24.310 --> 01:03:25.700
what are your thoughts
on the school of thought

01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:29.170
that say that moving toward electric,

01:03:29.170 --> 01:03:32.940
usually produces batteries,
which also produce waste,

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:36.160
and also increases the
demand for certain ships

01:03:36.160 --> 01:03:38.670
that are needed for these
kind of electric cars,

01:03:38.670 --> 01:03:43.159
mainly are mined from the, (indistinct)

01:03:43.159 --> 01:03:46.200
which also has been made with some form

01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:50.030
of child labor violations.

01:03:50.030 --> 01:03:53.650
So what are your thoughts
regarding the reconciliation,

01:03:53.650 --> 01:03:55.820
because now moving towards electric,

01:03:55.820 --> 01:03:59.017
some people may see it more
like any other privilege,

01:03:59.017 --> 01:04:02.529
and also (indistinct)

01:04:02.529 --> 01:04:06.560
more electric battery
waste that in the long run,

01:04:06.560 --> 01:04:09.630
it kind of what thoughts had been that

01:04:09.630 --> 01:04:13.477
it kind reflected back to
these lower income countries

01:04:13.477 --> 01:04:16.040
that already are buried in the branch

01:04:16.040 --> 01:04:17.737
of like the first hand (indistinct).

01:04:19.051 --> 01:04:21.400
How do you tackle (indistinct)?

01:04:21.400 --> 01:04:23.933
- Very legitimate and important point.

01:04:25.140 --> 01:04:28.720
I have been to many oil-rich countries

01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:31.663
in Africa in particular,

01:04:32.690 --> 01:04:37.690
and I've seen vast income
with very little benefit

01:04:38.470 --> 01:04:43.160
to the people, Angola, Gabon, Nigeria.

01:04:43.160 --> 01:04:47.940
I've seen vast exploitation
of humans in the oil industry,

01:04:47.940 --> 01:04:52.260
and I've also seen a
tremendous pollution impact

01:04:52.260 --> 01:04:53.450
in these settings.

01:04:53.450 --> 01:04:57.010
So I'm not saying that
we don't have a problem

01:04:57.010 --> 01:04:59.750
with rare earths and minerals

01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:02.250
that are required for nickel and the like

01:05:02.250 --> 01:05:03.872
in battery production.

01:05:03.872 --> 01:05:06.130
Congo is one of the major producers.

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:09.020
China is another one
of the major producers.

01:05:09.020 --> 01:05:14.020
So I think, yes, we have
a problem in consumption

01:05:15.976 --> 01:05:20.976
of products that are often yielded

01:05:21.840 --> 01:05:24.570
from an environment like Russia,

01:05:24.570 --> 01:05:28.320
which now uses its oil money
to wage war on the Ukrainians.

01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:33.320
So there is a challenge on
both sides of the equation

01:05:34.640 --> 01:05:39.640
on the battery power
needed for electric cars

01:05:41.320 --> 01:05:44.210
and for the oil and gas needed

01:05:44.210 --> 01:05:49.210
for the fossil fuel consuming cars.

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:55.530
So I think one would need
a more complex analysis,

01:05:55.530 --> 01:05:59.270
shall we say, of overall harms.

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:04.270
Now, can we demand that
Tesla and GM and Ford

01:06:06.080 --> 01:06:10.200
source their materials the
way that we've tried to demand

01:06:10.200 --> 01:06:13.390
that global diamond manufacturers
source their materials.

01:06:13.390 --> 01:06:16.530
The blood diamonds are not permitted

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:19.080
and they have to be tracked, et cetera.

01:06:19.080 --> 01:06:22.140
So there may be mitigating elements

01:06:22.140 --> 01:06:25.120
that we can do in the
sourcing of these materials

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:26.730
so we don't have the choice

01:06:26.730 --> 01:06:30.100
between abusing Congolese children

01:06:30.100 --> 01:06:33.990
and climate change-related catastrophe

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:34.830
50 years from now.

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:37.260
So I think we have to resolve,

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:40.330
I have to recognize them as you have done,

01:06:40.330 --> 01:06:43.370
address them, and resolve them.

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:46.557
And that would be my
choice rather than say,

01:06:46.557 --> 01:06:49.910
"Eh, forget electric, we'll
go with the gas stuff,

01:06:49.910 --> 01:06:52.450
'cause it won't hurt Congolese children."

01:06:52.450 --> 01:06:54.100
Well, it's gonna hurt Nigerian children,

01:06:54.100 --> 01:06:57.280
it's gonna hurt Gabonese
children and Angola children.

01:06:57.280 --> 01:07:00.580
So I'm not saying it's a wash

01:07:00.580 --> 01:07:03.200
'cause I'm not expert enough
in the topic to comment,

01:07:03.200 --> 01:07:06.120
but that would be the way
I would think about it.

01:07:06.120 --> 01:07:06.953
Thank you.

01:07:09.410 --> 01:07:12.120
